SplatAD is itself a fork of gsplat (https://github.com/nerfstudio-project/gsplat),
also Apache-2.0.

This repository contains a MODIFIED subset of that work. Changes made:

  * Reduced to the LiDAR path. The camera/RGB rasterizer, training strategies,
    compression, distributed helpers and profiling utilities were removed; what
    remains is the spherical LiDAR projection, tile binning and point
    rasterization plus the code they need.
  * Rebased onto gsplat 1.5.3 for everything that is not LiDAR-specific. The
    device math the two paths share -- quat_to_rotmat, quat_scale_to_covar_preci,
    posW2C, covarW2C, add_blur, inverse_vjp, the glm warpSum overloads, the
    CHECK_*/DEVICE_GUARD/CUB_WRAPPER macros and the glm typedefs -- is now
    #included from the installed gsplat's headers (Utils.cuh, Common.h) instead
    of being carried as a fork of gsplat 1.0.0. Only SplatAD's own spherical
    math is kept: lidar_proj, depth_compensation_from_cov3d, vel_world_to_cam,
    angle_difference, the 2x2 inverse gsplat 1.5.3 no longer has, and the
    float2/float3 warpSum overloads it never had.
    One behavioural note: gsplat 1.5.3 floors the antialiasing compensation at
    MIN_COMPENSATION^2 where 1.0.0 floored it at zero. That value is only
    computed when calc_compensations is requested, which the LiDAR path does not
    do, so outputs are unchanged -- verified bit-exact on a 5-sensor rig over
    six frames.
  * Python package renamed  gsplat -> splatad_kernel, and the CUDA extension
    renamed  gsplat_cuda -> splatad_kernel_cuda, so this can be installed
    alongside an ordinary gsplat without either colliding.
  * Inference-side performance work on the LiDAR rasterizer and tile binner
    (shared-memory staging and packing, an exponential-free rejection test, an
    elevation fold for one-beam-row tiles, and per-beam rather than per-bounding
    box azimuth binning). See the git history for what each change was measured
    to be worth.
  * Rolling shutter reached through the existing per-column sweep-time input.

